    "title": "PTSD Checklist for DSM-5 (PCL-5)",
    "about": "Twenty questions about problems after a very stressful experience, in the past month, each answered from 0 (not at all) to 4 (extremely), scored 0 to 80. A total of 31 or more suggests probable PTSD (Bovin 2016); confirm with a clinical assessment. For a positive PC-PTSD-5. The VA intends it for use by qualified health professionals.",
    "instructions": "Below is a list of problems that people sometimes have in response to a very stressful experience. Keeping your worst event in mind, please read each problem carefully and then select one of the numbers to the right to indicate how much you have been bothered by that problem in the past month.",
    "licence": {
        "holder": "U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs, National Center for PTSD",
        "terms": "Developed by staff at VA's National Center for PTSD; in the public domain and not copyrighted. Intended for use by qualified health professionals and researchers. Cite: Weathers FW, Litz BT, Keane TM, Palmieri PA, Marx BP, Schnurr PP. The PTSD Checklist for DSM-5 (PCL-5). National Center for PTSD, 2013.",
        "permission": "None needed: the VA states it is in the public domain (ptsd.va.gov)."
    },
    "source": {
        "name": "PCL-5, Past Month, VA National Center for PTSD (version date 29 August 2023)",
        "url": "https://www.ptsd.va.gov/professional/assessment/adult-sr/ptsd-checklist.asp"
    },
    "cutoffs": [
        {
            "score": 31,
            "meaning": "probable PTSD (Bovin 2016, against the CAPS-5); confirm with a clinical assessment"
        }
    ],
    "cutoffs_source": {
        "citation": "Bovin MJ, Marx BP, Weathers FW, et al. Psychometric properties of the PTSD Checklist for Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders-Fifth Edition (PCL-5) in veterans. Psychol Assess. 2016;28(11):1379-1391.",
        "short": "Bovin 2016",
        "pmid": "26653052",
        "doi": "10.1037/pas0000254",
        "stated": "Abstract: \"Signal detection analyses using the CAPS-5 revealed that PCL-5 scores of 31 to 33 were optimally efficient for diagnosing PTSD\"; studied in veterans. The lowest of the three, 31, is printed."
    },
    "accepted": {
        "questions": {
            "9": "The VA's printed item 9 gives examples (\"for example, having thoughts such as: I am bad, there is something seriously wrong with me, no one can be trusted, the world is completely dangerous\"); LOINC's item shortens it to \"Having strong negative beliefs about yourself, others, or the world?\".",
            "17": "The VA's form puts \"superalert\" in quotation marks; LOINC's item has none."
        }
    },
